Title: corpus_filter --skip-llm does not neutralise an inherited llm_irrelevant column
Created: 2026-07-27
Author: HDMX-coding-agent

--- log ---
2026-07-27T10:12Z HDMX-coding-agent created
2026-07-27T10:22Z HDMX-coding-agent note from the independent review of PR #1127 (ticket 0314, verdict APPROVED); the reviewer found this beyond the four challenge points it was given.

--- body ---
## Context

Ticket 0314 closed the 2026-07-24 incident: Flag 6 could be skipped silently
and ship an under-filtered corpus. The fix makes an unrunnable Flag 6 raise,
and makes `run_flagging` seed `llm_irrelevant` from the current run rather
than inheriting it from `extended_works.csv`.

That guarantees freshness **when Flag 6 runs**. It does not cover the other
path. `is_flagged` in the apply/audit functions (`scripts/harvest/
corpus_filter.py`, around lines 233, 273 and 377) computes membership from
whatever `FLAG_COLUMNS` are present in the frame, with no reference to
`--skip-llm`. So a run with `--skip-llm`, over an input that already carries
an `llm_irrelevant` column from a previous pass, lets that stale column drive
removal decisions.

The failure direction is opposite to the original incident: stale
**over**-removal rather than silent under-removal. It is also arguably the
intended semantics — "skip" plausibly means "do not recompute", not "do not
use". What it is not is *stated*: nothing in the CLI help, the code, or
0314's exit criteria says which reading applies, and the two readings produce
different corpora from the same command.

## Relevant files

- `scripts/harvest/corpus_filter.py` — `is_flagged` in the apply/audit paths;
  `expected_flag_columns()` already knows about `--skip-llm` and is the
  natural place for the decision to live
- `tests/test_corpus_filter.py` — `TestFlag6NotCarriedOver` covers the
  Flag-6-runs path; the `--skip-llm` path has no equivalent

## Actions

1. Decide the semantics first, and write it down: does `--skip-llm` mean
   "reuse whatever is there" or "this run makes no llm_irrelevant claim"?
   Do not change behaviour before answering — both readings are defensible
   and the current code picks one silently.
2. Implement the chosen reading explicitly rather than by omission. If
   "reuse", say so in the CLI help and log which pass the column came from.
   If "ignore", drop the column from the membership computation under
   `--skip-llm`.
3. Either way the log line should state what happened, in the spirit of the
   0314 fix: a corpus built with a reused flag column is fine if it is known.

## Test

- Red first: run the filter with `--skip-llm` over a fixture carrying a
  populated `llm_irrelevant` column, and assert the chosen semantics on the
  refined output row count.

## Verification

- [ ] `--skip-llm` semantics stated in one place and enforced by a test
- [ ] The run log names which pass any reused flag column came from

## Invariants

- Do not weaken 0314: an unrunnable Flag 6 must still be a hard error.
- No silent change of corpus membership between runs with identical flags.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The two readings can no longer produce different corpora from the same
      command without the log saying so
